e\":false,\"permanent\":false,\"direction\":\"auto\",\"opacity\":1,\"offset\":[0,0],\"textsize\":\"10px\",\"textOnly\":false,\"className\":\"\",\"sticky\":true},null]},{\"method\":\"addCircleMarkers\",\"args\":[[-22.72463163,-17.875,-15.625,-14.625,-14.375,-14.125,-12.125,-11.125,-9.875,-9.375,-8.625,-7.375,-7.125,-6.875,-6.125,-5.875,-5.125,-4.625,0.875,1.625,2.375,3.125,3.875,4.875,5.125,5.625,5.875,7.875,8.625,9.125,9.125,9.375,9.375,9.625,9.875,10.125,10.625,10.875,10.875],[28.76857325,26.875,25.875,26.875,27.125,26.875,26.625,26.375,26.125,26.375,26.375,26.125,26.125,26.125,25.125,24.875,24.875,24.625,23.125,23.625,23.625,23.875,23.125,23.125,24.125,24.125,23.875,24.875,25.125,25.375,25.875,26.125,27.125,27.375,27.625,27.875,28.125,28.625,28.625],[11.0359746709628,3.711378604756677,3.95945148134785,4.068511591897543,4.231775965010547,7.615532223977366,3.973894262569155,5.435473017877149,4.15847792399318,6.510763432191359,4.766981179783293,4.216089382099366,4.290188389066315,6.715652410310211,4.098994289225715,4.869023179871697,5.826070991131925,5.82737810509262,4.020061538313922,5.790421432032999,4.265820468202227,6.93875912688052,3.926532477971708,4.138864025385446,4.061668170753292,3.118527670592318,3.889247828153915,4.119037697752423,3.072033412079122,4.808483000632855,4.247329270330857,4.122357033247872,5.533170905478096,3.991788960528579,4.375898599586339,4.871227410149064,4.929759988648392,4.219237470620987,11.04022238685532],null,null,{\"interactive\":true,\"className\":\"\",\"stroke\":true,\"color\":\"#999999\",\"weight\":5,\"opacity\":0.5,\"fill\":true,\"fillColor\":\"#999999\",\"fillOpacity\":0.8},null,null,[\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #1\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #2\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #3\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #4\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #5\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #6\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #7\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #8\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #9\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #10\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #11\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #12\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #13\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #14\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #15\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #16\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #17\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #18\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #19\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #20\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #21\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #22\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #23\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #24\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #25\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #26\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #27\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #28\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #29\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #30\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #31\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #32\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #33\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #34\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #35\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #36\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #37\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #38\",\"Tag ID: 22NO<br>stap #39\"],null,null,{\"interactive\":false,\"permanent\":false,\"direction\":\"auto\",\"opacity\":1,\"offset\":[0,0],\"textsize\":\"10px\",\"textOnly\":false,\"className\":\"\",\"sticky\":true},null]},{\"method\":\"addLegend\",\"args\":[{\"colors\":[\"#E41A1C\",\"#4DAF4A\",\"#FF7F00\",\"#A65628\",\"#999999\"],\"labels\":[\"16LN\",\"16LO\",\"16LP\",\"20IK\",\"22NO\"],\"na_color\":null,\"na_label\":\"NA\",\"opacity\":1,\"position\":\"topright\",\"type\":\"factor\",\"title\":\"Bird Trajectories\",\"extra\":null,\"layerId\":null,\"className\":\"info legend\",\"group\":null}]}],\"limits\":{\"lat\":[-22.875,10.875],\"lng\":[22.625,29.875]}},\"evals\":[],\"jsHooks\":[]}</script>\n```\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nEach table has an accessor returning a tibble: `tags()`, `observations()`, `measurements()`, `staps()`, `paths()`, `edges()`, `twilights()` and `pressurepaths()`. Their content is described in [Publish your data](geolocator-create.qmd) and on the [GeoLocator DP website](https://geopressure.org/GeoLocator-DP/).\n\n## Keys and joins\n\nThe tables of a package are linked by keys, like a relational database. The [schema overview](https://geopressure.org/GeoLocator-DP/schema-overview/) draws them all.\n\n- A **primary key** identifies each row. In `staps`, it is the pair `tag_id` and `stap_id`: stationary period 3 only means something for a given tag.\n- A **foreign key** points to the primary key of another table. `paths` and `edges` point to `staps`, and all tables point to `tags` through `tag_id`.\n\nThe trajectory tables add two columns to their primary key:\n\n| Table | Primary key |\n|---|---|\n| `paths` | `tag_id`, `type`, `j`, `stap_id` |\n| `edges` | `tag_id`, `type`, `j`, `stap_s` |\n| `pressurepaths` | `tag_id`, `type`, `j`, `datetime` |\n\n`type` is `most_likely`, the single best trajectory (`j = 1`); `simulation`, random draws from the posterior distribution (`j = 1` to `nj`) that carry the location uncertainty; `geopressureviz`, a path edited by hand in `geopressureviz()`; or `tag`, the most likely position of each stationary period taken separately from its likelihood map, without the movement model. **Always join trajectory tables on `type` and `j`**, or you will mix trajectories.\n\nIn dplyr, a join takes the keys in `by`. When the columns have different names, `join_by()` maps them. For example, to get the position where each flight started, join `edges` to `paths` on the departure stationary period `stap_s`:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nedges(pkg) |>\n  left_join(paths(pkg), by = join_by(tag_id, type, j, stap_s == stap_id)) |>\n  select(tag_id, type, j, stap_s, stap_t, lat, lon, distance) |>\n  head() |>\n  knitr::kable(digits = 1)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|tag_id |type        |  j| stap_s| stap_t|   lat|  lon| distance|\n|:------|:-----------|--:|------:|------:|-----:|----:|--------:|\n|16LN   |most_likely |  1|      1|      2| -22.7| 28.8|    363.6|\n|16LN   |most_likely |  1|      2|      3| -20.1| 26.6|    140.8|\n|16LN   |most_likely |  1|      3|      4| -18.9| 26.4|    114.7|\n|16LN   |most_likely |  1|      4|      5| -18.1| 27.1|    122.7|\n|16LN   |most_likely |  1|      5|      6| -17.1| 26.6|     79.8|\n|16LN   |most_likely |  1|      6|      7| -17.1| 27.4|    250.0|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe same keys tell you how to aggregate. The total distance flown is a sum over the flights of **one** trajectory, so we group by `tag_id`, `type` and `j`, then summarise the simulations of each tag:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nedges(pkg) |>\n  summarise(km = sum(distance), .by = c(tag_id, type, j)) |>\n  summarise(\n    most_likely = km[type == \"most_likely\"],\n    median = median(km[type == \"simulation\"]),\n    lower = quantile(km[type == \"simulation\"], 0.05),\n    upper = quantile(km[type == \"simulation\"], 0.95),\n    .by = tag_id\n  ) |>\n  knitr::kable(digits = 0, col.names = c(\"Tag\", \"Most likely (km)\", \"Simulations (km)\", \"5%\", \"95%\"))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n\n\n|Tag  | Most likely (km)| Simulations (km)|   5%|   95%|\n|:----|----------------:|----------------:|----:|-----:|\n|16LN |             8243|             9643| 9094| 10346|\n|16LO |             8813|             9900| 9225| 10611|\n|16LP |             7794|             9033| 8463|  9770|\n|20IK |             8465|            10336| 9711| 10906|\n|22NO |             4393|             5129| 4780|  5631|\n\n\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe most likely trajectory is shorter than every simulation: it is the best position at each stationary period, so it smooths out the zig-zags that the simulations draw within the location uncertainty. Use the simulations for quantities summed along a trajectory.\n\n## Compare the birds of a study\n\nBecause all tags share the same tables, figures across birds are a few joins away. Here, the latitude of each bird through the year, expanded to one value per day from its stationary periods, with the 90% interval of the simulations:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\nlatitude <- paths(pkg) |>\n  summarise(\n    lower = quantile(lat[type == \"simulation\"], 0.05),\n    upper = quantile(lat[type == \"simulation\"], 0.95),\n    lat = lat[type == \"most_likely\"],\n    .by = c(tag_id, stap_id)\n  ) |>\n  inner_join(staps(pkg), by = c(\"tag_id\", \"stap_id\")) |>\n  reframe(\n    date = seq(as.Date(start), as.Date(end), by = \"day\"),\n    .by = c(tag_id, stap_id, lat, lower, upper)\n  ) |>\n  distinct(tag_id, date, .keep_all = TRUE)\n```\n:::\n\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code  code-fold=\"true\"}\nlatitude |>\n  mutate(doy = yday(date), year = year(date)) |>\n  ggplot(aes(doy, group = interaction(tag_id, year), colour = tag_id, fill = tag_id)) +\n  geom_hline(yintercept = 0, colour = \"grey60\", linetype = \"dashed\") +\n  geom_ribbon(aes(ymin = lower, ymax = upper), alpha = 0.15, colour = NA) +\n  geom_line(aes(y = lat), linewidth = 0.8) +\n  scale_x_continuous(\n    breaks = c(1, 32, 60, 91, 121, 152, 182, 213, 244, 274, 305, 335),\n    labels = month.abb, expand = expansion(mult = 0.01)\n  ) +\n  scale_colour_brewer(palette = \"Dark2\", aesthetics = c(\"colour\", \"fill\")) +\n  labs(\n    x = NULL, y = \"Latitude\", colour = \"Tag\", fill = \"Tag\",\n    title = \"Woodland Kingfishers spend the austral winter north of the equator\"\n  ) +\n  theme(legend.position = \"bottom\", plot.title.position = \"plot\", plot.title = element_text(face = \"bold\"))\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output-display}\n![](geolocator-read_files/figure-html/16-daily-latitude-plot-1.png){width=672}\n:::\n:::\n\n\nAll five birds leave South Africa between late March and mid-April, spend the austral winter around 8–11°N, and are back by early December.\n\n## Back to GeoPressureR\n\nA package keeps the raw measurements and the labels of the original analysis. `gldp_to_tag()` converts them into a GeoPressureR [tag object](tag-object.qmd), with its labelled pressure, its stationary periods and its twilights:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ntag <- gldp_to_tag(pkg, tag_id = \"16LP\")\ntag\n```\n:::\n\n\nFrom here, the tag works with every chapter of this manual. For instance, you can recompute its [pressure map](pressure-map.qmd) with different settings, add a [light map](light-map.qmd), or build a [trajectory with wind](trajectory-with-wind.qmd). Without `tag_id`, `gldp_to_tag()` returns a named list with one tag per bird.\n\nTo re-run the whole study, `create_geopressuretemplate()` creates a [GeoPressureTemplate](geopressuretemplate-intro.qmd) project with the data, labels and parameters of all tags:\n\n\n::: {.cell}\n\n```{.r .cell-code}\ncreate_geopressuretemplate(path = \"~/woodlandkingfisher\", pkg = pkg)\n```\n:::\n\n\n## Licence, embargo and contact\n\n`pkg$licenses` gives the licence and `pkg$bibliographicCitation` the citation, with the concept DOI: cite it, and record the version you used (`pkg$id`, e.g. in your methods). Read the record's description too: authors often ask to be contacted, and they know the caveats of their data. `pkg$contributors` lists their roles: write to the contact person. An embargoed record has public metadata but no files: `read_zenodo()` warns and returns the metadata only. Ask its authors for access, and do not redistribute data obtained this way.\n\n## Compare studies\n\nTo compare studies, do not download and merge packages yourself: the **Master GeoLocator Data Package** already compiles them all into one versioned dataset. See [Compare studies with the Master](geolocator-master.qmd).\n",
